          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nGeneralized anxiety disorder (GAD) is a condition of persistent, excessive worry about a range of everyday matters. The worry happens on more days than not for at least six months, and the person finds it hard to control. It is usually accompanied by physical and mental symptoms: restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. *(Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder,\" General clinical reference.)*\n\nOrdinary worry and GAD differ in a few ways, according to the same source:\n\n- **Breadth:** GAD worry moves from topic to topic rather than staying attached to one situation.\n- **Proportion:** The worry is out of step with the circumstances.\n- **Persistence:** It continues even when the circumstances change.\n- **Impact:** It causes real difficulty with daily life.\n\nAnxiety itself is a normal and useful response to threat. Feeling anxious does not mean someone has GAD.\n\nScreening guidance also mentions a seven-item questionnaire often used to measure anxiety severity. A score of 10 or more is the usual point at which further assessment follows. *(Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care,\" Preventive services and psychiatric association guidance.)* The same guidance notes that the evidence on screening older adults is insufficient to recommend either way.\n\nThe sources I found do not say how GAD is formally diagnosed, what treatments are available, or whether any of these features apply to a particular person. If you are concerned about persistent worry in your own life, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to talk to. They can assess it properly.",
